A generative adversarial network (GAN) is a machine‑learning framework in which two neural networks—the generator and the discriminator—compete in a zero‑sum game. The generator creates data samples, while the discriminator evaluates how realistic they are. The generator learns to fool the discriminator, enabling the model to produce new data that mimics the training set’s statistics. (Source: genai_gan.pdf, page 1)

```java
// Simple shopping site skeleton
import java.util.*;

public class ShoppingSite {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        Scanner sc = new Scanner(System.in);
        Map<String, Double> catalog = new HashMap<>();
        catalog.put("Laptop", 999.99);
        catalog.put("Phone", 599.99);
        catalog.put("Headphones", 199.99);

        System.out.println("Welcome to the Shopping Site!");
        System.out.println("Available items:");
        catalog.forEach((k,v)-> System.out.println(k + " - $" + v));

        System.out.print("Enter item to buy: ");
        String item = sc.nextLine();
        if (catalog.containsKey(item)) {
            System.out.println("You bought a " + item + " for $" + catalog.get(item));
        } else {
            System.out.println("Item not found.");
        }
        sc.close();
    }
}
```
